-<p>Year one is a paid experiment beside the job, not owner income. It loses €23,600 after valuing founder time. Cash loss is lower because that labour is not a payroll cheque, but ignoring it would disguise a weak practice. The model has passed only when customers pay both stages without a goods margin subsidising them.</p>
-<p>The mature hurdle assumes ten completed orders a month. At twenty-four delivery hours per order, delivery consumes 240 hours monthly. Two coordinators and one quality lead each provide a designed 120 productive hours, leaving one-third of capacity for exceptions, training and absences. The founder sells, handles escalations and maintains supplier relationships. If average delivery exceeds thirty hours, gross margin falls below the intended level before overhead.</p>
-<p>The €100,000 founder line is replacement-cost compensation before personal tax. The €120,000 operating surplus is separate and must fund tax, hiring variance and retained capital. There is no recurring revenue in the base case. Repeat projects and supplier familiarity may lower acquisition and delivery cost, but each fee must be earned on an order.</p>
+<p>Year one is a paid experiment beside the job, not owner income. Before founder compensation, it produces a €6,400 cash operating result: €28,000 revenue less €15,600 delivery cost and €6,000 overhead. After valuing founder time, it loses €23,600. Ignoring that labour would disguise a weak practice. The model passes only when customers pay both stages without a goods margin subsidising them.</p>
+<p>The external €500 review is a Year-one design hurdle for one bounded Dutch review per order, outside the founder-hour line. At maturity, the employed quality lead performs that work; there is no second €60,000 review charge. The twenty-four-hour mature target allocates sixteen coordinator hours and eight quality-lead hours per order. All are planning hurdles, not observed costs.</p>
+<p>The mature hurdle assumes ten completed orders a month, consuming 240 of 360 designed productive hours. Two coordinators and one quality lead each supply 120 hours. At thirty hours per order, the contingency buffer halves; at thirty-six, delivery consumes all capacity. The table’s gross margin does not change unless overtime or another hire becomes necessary. The founder sells, handles escalations and maintains supplier relationships.</p>
+<p>The €100,000 founder line is replacement-cost compensation before personal tax. The €180,000 operating surplus is separate and must fund tax, hiring variance and retained capital. There is no recurring revenue in the base case. Repeat projects and supplier familiarity may lower acquisition and delivery cost, but each fee must be earned on an order.</p>
 <p>Working capital is controlled by refusing title to the goods, billing each service stage 50% in advance and using contractors until receipts support employment. Before hiring, use a €25,000 reserve hurdle for late customer payments, rework and professional fees. Year-one cash at risk stays below €50,000 only if no factory deposit, freight bill or warranty claim enters the company. Crossing that boundary changes the business into a distributor and requires a new liability and capital case.</p>
